{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_bgnxhw2ojjmgo2fn7dh6vplz45f6oq63wwljytcrd3t5gc52xl2a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01QUCXFvPcpov7L2994aW5xA","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-OyL4sR/translated_rust/tests/differential.rs","old_string":"    /// Lets the harness see the *size of the allocation*, not just its\n    /// contents. Without this, a Rust translation that requested a different\n    /// (e.g. too small) buffer would still compare equal, because the encoded\n    /// bytes written past the end of a short block happen to read back fine.\n    /// glibc rounds a request up to a 16-byte granularity, so this detects any\n    /// divergence that crosses a bucket boundary — which randomized sizes hit\n    /// with probability ~1/16 per case, i.e. certainty across a whole test.\n    fn malloc_usable_size(ptr: *mut c_void) -> usize;","new_string":"    /// Used for a **one-sided** invariant only: the returned block must be big\n    /// enough for the encoded output plus its terminating NUL.\n    ///\n    /// It deliberately is NOT used to compare the two allocations for equality:\n    /// glibc does not always hand back an exact-fit chunk (it may serve a\n    /// request from a larger free chunk when splitting is not profitable), so\n    /// `malloc_usable_size` is not a function of the requested size and an\n    /// equality assertion on it is flaky in both directions. Undersized\n    /// allocations are instead caught by this lower bound plus the\n    /// `MALLOC_CHECK_`/`MALLOC_PERTURB_` run documented in VERIFICATION.md.\n    fn malloc_usable_size(ptr: *mut c_void) -> usize;"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2288,"cache_read_input_tokens":100163,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2288,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0}